WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.280
Every signal the brain gets from the eye leaves through ganglion cells, whose axons

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.520
form the optic nerve.

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.600
But the optic nerve is not a camera cable.

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.400
A mouse has more than 40 types of ganglion cell, and each type sends the brain a

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.480
different feature of the scene.

6
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.480
Some signal local differences in brightness.

7
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.160
Others detect a small moving object, or motion in one direction.

8
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.300
Circuits inside the retina build each feature before any spike leaves the eye.

9
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:40.200
Take the W3 cell. It pools excitation from the ON and OFF pathways, so a local

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.080
change of either sign excites it.

11
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.660
Much of that excitation arrives through an excitatory amacrine cell.

12
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.980
When the distant surround moves too, the circuit suppresses that excitation and

13
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:52.980
inhibits the cell.

14
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.940
So W3 fires for a small object moving against a still background, and falls quiet

15
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.080
when the whole scene moves.

16
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.080
. . . .

17
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:07.340
A second channel is the JAM-B cell, an OFF cell with a lopsided dendritic arbor.

18
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.680
These asymmetric cells preferred one direction of motion, from the cell body toward

19
00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.200
the dendrites. Other channels report contrast, colour, or light level.

20
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.100
Information that no channel carries is gone before the brain has a chance.

21
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:25.193
How do we know a channel is a real type?

22
00:01:26.013 --> 00:01:30.693
Three kinds of evidence can define one: a shared light response, dendritic shape,

23
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:32.293
and gene expression profile.

24
00:01:33.173 --> 00:01:34.653
A cluster is not yet a type.

25
00:01:35.453 --> 00:01:39.653
A type is supported when these agree, and when its cells are spaced in a regular

26
00:01:39.653 --> 00:01:43.013
mosaic, though some real types are only weakly regular.

27
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:46.297
Each method counts by its own evidence.

28
00:01:47.437 --> 00:01:51.137
Imaging grouped cells by light response and found at least 32 types.

29
00:01:52.097 --> 00:01:53.277
Sequencing found 46.

30
00:01:54.557 --> 00:01:56.637
Recording from single cells found 42.

31
00:01:57.737 --> 00:02:00.217
Electron microscopy supported at least 35.

32
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:02.597
These are not rival answers.

33
00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:08.537
Each reflects a method and a resolution, and the authors read them as converging on

34
00:02:08.537 --> 00:02:09.537
about 45.

35
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:11.967
Mouse is not macaque.

36
00:02:12.807 --> 00:02:17.567
The primate fovea is packed with midget ganglion cells, each fed through a bipolar

37
00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:21.627
cell by a single cone, and that private line supports sharp vision.

38
00:02:22.587 --> 00:02:28.127
In the macaque fovea, midget clusters made up about 85% % of sampled ganglion cells.

39
00:02:29.247 --> 00:02:33.667
Shared ancestry with mouse cells does not mean a shared size, frequency, or job.

40
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.800
So the eye does not send the brain a picture.

41
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.940
It sends dozens of filtered versions of the scene, side by side, and the brain can

42
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.880
only work with what those channels deliver.
